WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.720
DAVID TU: Hey.

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:01.680
This is David Tu.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.080
I'm a network specialist
at Google Cloud.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.443
Welcome back to What's
New in Networking.

00:00:06.443 --> 00:00:08.610
Today we'll guide you through
our new load balancing

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.680
and service networking launches.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.920
First our new features around
external and internal load

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.090
balancers.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:18.510
Next we'll take a quick stop
discussing Traffic Director.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:21.330
And we'll finish off our journey
with Private Service Connect.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:22.050
Stay tuned.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:30.415
First stop is our External
Network Load Balancer.

00:00:30.415 --> 00:00:32.790
The Network Load Balancer is
a regional pass-through load

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:35.520
balancer that
distributes TCP and UDP

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.520
traffic among virtual
machines in the same region.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.470
As you might know, the
network load balancer

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.470
leverages target pools
or target instances.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.570
With the recent launch,
we've enabled the use

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.310
of regional back-end services.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.650
Similar to target pools,
a back end service

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:53.230
defines how cloud load
balancing distributes traffic.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.590
But now backend
service configurations

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.690
give you fine-grained
control over how

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.310
your load balancers behave.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:02.430
It contains a set of values
like protocols, distribution

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:06.000
and settings, health
checks, and timeouts.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.780
So what does this launch mean?

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:09.900
First, this launch
gets feature parity

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.230
for the same backend services
as other Google Cloud Load

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:17.370
Balancers, like our Global Load
Balancer or our TCP and SSL

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:18.900
proxy load balancer.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.360
Second, it transitions
from legacy health checks

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.700
to unified health
checks, which allows you

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.620
to configure TCP health checks.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.990
Third, it enables
connection draining.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.900
And lastly, it enables
failover groups.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.820
All right, now let's
move on to what's

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.550
new with the internal
network load balancer.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.350
Prior to this launch,
if you were ever

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:40.230
required to send traffic to a
single internal load balancer

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:44.070
virtual IP, you were limited
to only one forwarding rule

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.140
per virtual IP address.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.570
With this launch, you'll
now be able to configure

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.930
multiple forwarding rules
with the same virtual IP.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:53.790
Now you'll have the
flexibility to define

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:59.040
different protocols, such as TCP
or UDP, to the same virtual IP.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.020
In addition, you
have the flexibility

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:04.830
to have multiple forwarding
rules to the same virtual IP,

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:06.900
but exposing different ports.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:08.550
This launch gives
you the ability

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:11.310
to have various forwarding
rules combinations pointing

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.500
to the same virtual IP.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.300
Now that we've covered
our load balancers,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.390
let's cover two new additions
to Traffic Director,

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.530
our fully managed traffic
control plane for service mesh.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:26.520
Introducing TCP services
support for Traffic Director.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.190
This feature enables you to
use Traffic Director to route

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:32.400
traffic to services that
expect non-HTTP traffic,

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.450
such as databases or
voice over IP services.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.820
Those of you who have
TCP-based services

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:42.390
get global load balancing
for multi-region services,

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:46.200
such as cross regional
failover and overflow,

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.040
a fully managed control
plane for TCP traffic,

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:54.570
a consistent control plane
across L4 and L7 traffic,

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:58.470
and you can easily connect
workloads to external services.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:01.410
For those of you that use
proxyless gRPC with Traffic

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:03.660
Director and need
additional visibility,

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.360
now you can, with observability
for proxyless gRPC.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.080
You're able to access
monitoring and tracing

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.480
metrics that help you solve
configuration and networking

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.880
issues with your deployment.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.610
Best of all, you can export
these metrics to cloud

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:20.010
monitoring and cloud trace.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.380
You can then build
dashboards and charts

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:25.998
and even use them as a
basis for automating alerts.

00:03:25.998 --> 00:03:27.790
Our last feature is
Private Service Connect

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:29.730
for Google APIs.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.620
Prior to this launch,
there were two ways

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.690
of connecting to Google APIs.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.340
The first is over
the public internet.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.670
This requires your client
to have a public IP

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:42.840
or go through NAT to hit
a public API endpoint.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:46.020
The second was to configure
private Google access.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.060
Private Google access
removed the requirement

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:51.150
to have a public IP
or go through NAT.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:53.310
Now your clients can
privately route traffic

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:56.400
to private endpoint IPs
that Google defines.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.370
With the launch of
PSC from Google APIs,

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:01.620
you have the ability to
create multiple custom

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.630
endpoints of your choosing.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:07.800
Now you get more control over
which APIs hit which endpoint

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.430
and it remains completely
private to your VPC.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.250
And that's what's new in
networking for load balancing

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.050
and service networking.

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:18.180
Thanks for sticking
around till the end.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.459
I welcome you to start
using these features

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:22.230
and be sure to take
a look at the links

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.570
below for guides
on how to use them.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:27.780
Stay tuned for the next
What's New in Networking.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:31.130
